abstract: >-
  A tournament is doubly regular, or a Hadamard tournament, if every vertex
  has the same out-degree and every two vertices have the same number of
  common out-neighbours. In 1984 Ito proved that a vertex-transitive Hadamard
  tournament has order v = p t^2 for a prime p congruent to 3 mod 4, and
  raised the possibility that v is always a power of p. An Ito
  counterexample is a vertex-transitive Hadamard tournament whose order is
  not a prime power; none is known. The paper proves that for every bound B
  only finitely many orders of Ito counterexamples have largest fourth-power
  divisor at most B. If no fourth power of a prime divides v > 1, a
  vertex-transitive Hadamard tournament of order v exists only when v = p or
  v = p^3; it is then a Cayley tournament of a p-group, and for cube-free v
  it is the Paley tournament. If v = p^(2a+1) s^2 with s > 1 squarefree and
  prime to p, then a >= 4 and p <= 4a - 5. For 3 <= v < 10^5 a
  vertex-transitive Hadamard tournament of order v exists if and only if v
  is a prime power congruent to 3 mod 4. Novelty is bounded to the sources
  searched.
keywords:
  - Hadamard tournaments
  - doubly regular tournaments
  - skew Hadamard matrices
  - vertex-transitive
  - permutation groups
